</dependencies>

c)	Configure database connection properties.
Aim: To implement precise connection pool strings, dialect paths, schema lifecycle rules, and debug properties within the application configuration environment. 

Application Properties (src/main/resources/application.properties):
# Web Server Deployment Configuration Port
server.port=8080

# Relational In-Memory H2 Engine Database Interconnection Properties
spring.datasource.url=jdbc:h2:mem:testdb
spring.datasource.driverClassName=org.h2.Driver
spring.datasource.username=sa
spring.datasource.password=

# Hibernate Core Object-Relational Mapping Framework Strategy Drivers
spring.jpa.database-platform=org.hibernate.dialect.H2Dialect
spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=update
spring.jpa.show-sql=true

# Developer In-Browser Graphic Terminal System Interconnection Console
spring.h2.console.enabled=true


d)	Create an entity class (e.g., User).
Aim: To establish a database-mapped schema model layer utilizing standard Java Persistence API specification specifications. 

User Persistence Entity Structure (User.java):
package com.example.demo.model;

import jakarta.persistence.Entity;
import jakarta.persistence.GeneratedValue;
import jakarta.persistence.GenerationType;
import jakarta.persistence.Id;
import jakarta.persistence.Table;

@Entity // Designates this Java class object as a database table relation schema mapping
@Table(name = "users")
public class User {

    @Id // Explicitly markers this field instance variable as the relational Primary Key component
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;
    
    private String name;
    private String email;

    // Default No-Args Constructor required implicitly by the JPA Specification runtime
    public User() {}

    // Multi-argument field population constructor layer logic
    public User(String name, String email) {
        this.name = name;
        this.email = email;
    }

    // Encapsulated field Accessors and Mutators
    public Long getId() { return id; }
    public void setId(Long id) { this.id = id; }

    public String getName() { return name; }
    public void setName(String name) { this.name = name; }

    public String getEmail() { return email; }
    public void setEmail(String email) { this.email = email; }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "User{id=" + id + ", name='" + name + "', email='" + email + "'}";
    }
}

e)	 Create a repository interface (e.g., UserRepository).
Aim: To write a structural interface layer extending data abstractions to inherit standard out-of-the-box CRUD processing capabilities. 

Data Layer Persistence Abstraction Interface (UserRepository.java):
package com.example.demo.repository;

import com.example.demo.model.User;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Repository;

@Repository // Declares a dedicated abstract data manipulation persistence bean component route
public interface UserRepository extends JpaRepository<User, Long> {
    // Standard CRUD abstractions (save, findAll, findById, deleteById) are extended implicitly
}

